WEBVTT

00:00:08.052 --> 00:00:13.222
So that's our offer. We think it's a fair
one, with advantages for both sides.

00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:18.432
Yes, well, we're prepared to consider your
offer Ms Chan, if you can accept some conditions.

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:21.351
And subject to consideration by the board.

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:23.642
What are the conditions?

00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:29.532
Well, firstly the price you're proposing.
Would that be variable depending on currency

00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:34.572
fluctuations? The issue is that we're in an
unstable environment at the moment - the exchange

00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:36.172
rate could affect us negatively.

00:00:36.172 --> 00:00:37.331
Us too!

00:00:37.331 --> 00:00:40.611
True, but the problem is that we're tied to
the U.S. dollar.

00:00:40.611 --> 00:00:44.822
We could consider hedging against currency
in both directions.

00:00:44.822 --> 00:00:47.352
That would be acceptable.

00:00:47.352 --> 00:00:53.252
Another problem we may have is that of supply.
Our customers often need supply at short notice.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:58.642
If we do get large orders, we need to guarantee
delivery, so we need to stockpile. The difficulty

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:04.782
there is the capital outlay. How would you
feel about a partial offset against our sales?

00:01:04.782 --> 00:01:06.322
You mean a loan.

00:01:06.322 --> 00:01:07.681
I suppose so.

00:01:07.681 --> 00:01:12.851
Would you be agreeable to a deferred payment?
We can provide security of course.

00:01:12.851 --> 00:01:19.162
I think that would be acceptable. Unfortunately,
I would need to get Board approval for it.

00:01:19.162 --> 00:01:20.642
Of course.

00:01:20.642 --> 00:01:22.972
Then I think we might have a deal!

00:01:22.972 --> 00:01:33.621
In principle.
Time to celebrate!

00:01:33.621 --> 00:01:38.881
In negotiations, it's usual for each side
to have conditions that make the deal better,

00:01:38.881 --> 00:01:45.332
or safer for them. A condition is a change
in the terms of a deal which is necessary

00:01:45.332 --> 00:01:50.312
before one side or the other agrees.
Yes, well, we're prepared to consider your

00:01:50.312 --> 00:01:59.562
offer Ms Chan, if you can accept some conditions.
Notice that Victor uses language carefully.

00:01:59.562 --> 00:02:06.142
He doesn't say "we agree to your offer", he
says "we're prepared to consider your offer".

00:02:06.142 --> 00:02:11.672
He is signalling to the other side that there
is a chance for agreement by using the word

00:02:11.672 --> 00:02:14.982
consider, which means think about .

00:02:14.982 --> 00:02:21.482
He then makes this conditional by saying,
if you can accept some conditions. In English,

00:02:21.482 --> 00:02:26.182
using the word 'if' in this way is called
a conditional.

00:02:26.182 --> 00:02:33.392
Victor is saying, "We can consider your offer
if you can accept some conditions." One part

00:02:33.392 --> 00:02:37.982
of the sentence is conditional on, or depending
on the other.

00:02:37.982 --> 00:02:44.252
The negative is also true. If they can't accept
the conditions, Victor can't consider the

00:02:44.252 --> 00:02:45.422
offer.

00:02:45.422 --> 00:02:52.372
Notice that Sue adds: subject to consideration
by the board. 'Subject to' is another type

00:02:52.372 --> 00:03:00.122
of conditional phrase. She means, "We can
agree if the board agrees."

00:03:00.122 --> 00:03:06.852
'Subject to' is a legal phrase meaning 'only
if', or 'only after'. There are a number of

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:12.982
expressions you can use when giving a condition.
Try them after me:

00:03:12.982 --> 00:03:17.352
subject to the board's agreement.

00:03:17.352 --> 00:03:22.211
conditional on the board's agreement.

00:03:22.211 --> 00:03:26.432
providing that the board agrees.

00:03:26.432 --> 00:03:31.642
as long as the board agrees.

00:03:31.642 --> 00:03:41.292
on condition that the board agrees.
Let's look now at Victor's first condition.

00:03:41.292 --> 00:03:47.612
Firstly the price you're proposing. Would
that be variable depending on currency fluctuations?

00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:52.272
The issue is that we're in an unstable environment
at the moment - the exchange rate could affect

00:03:52.272 --> 00:03:56.232
us negatively.
When Victor is talking about conditions - he

00:03:56.232 --> 00:04:01.582
is exploring various scenarios - or things
that could happen.

00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:08.912
Notice that he explains what the problem is.
He says, "The issue is that we're in an unstable

00:04:08.912 --> 00:04:10.101
environment."

00:04:10.101 --> 00:04:15.162
'The issue' means the problem, or the thing
that needs discussing.

00:04:15.162 --> 00:04:19.911
Practise with Victor some ways of introducing
a problem.

00:04:19.911 --> 00:04:24.862
The issue is the exchange rate.

00:04:24.862 --> 00:04:29.761
The problem is the exchange rate.

00:04:29.761 --> 00:04:35.571
The difficulty we have is with the exchange
rate.

00:04:35.571 --> 00:04:40.541
One thing that could happen is that the exchange
rate, the amount of money you can exchange

00:04:40.541 --> 00:04:48.441
in one currency for another, may change. Notice
that Victor uses the words 'would' and 'could'.

00:04:48.441 --> 00:04:54.362
'Would' is like a conditional. One thing might
result in another thing happening.

00:04:54.362 --> 00:04:58.492
The exchange rate might change.

00:04:58.492 --> 00:05:03.881
Victor is talking about the price for their
product. He asks, "Would that be variable

00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:13.122
depending on currency fluctuations?"'Fluctuations
are changes. We can express this another way:

00:05:13.122 --> 00:05:21.721
If the currency changes, will the price change?
'Could' is used to express a possibility - something

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.511
that might happen.

00:05:24.511 --> 00:05:31.302
Victor says, "the exchange rate could affect
us negatively". Notice that you can say something

00:05:31.302 --> 00:05:37.462
will affect you negatively - it will have
a negative, or bad effect, or positively - it

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:41.112
will have a positive, or good effect.

00:05:41.112 --> 00:05:45.932
What is Lin's response to this first condition,
or concern of Victor's?

00:05:45.932 --> 00:05:50.141
We could consider hedging against currency
in both directions.

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:56.042
That would be acceptable.
Like Victor, Lin is being careful. She uses

00:05:56.042 --> 00:06:04.661
the word 'consider' rather than just agreeing.
She's waiting until the whole deal is clear.

00:06:04.661 --> 00:06:10.992
We can give both questions and answers using
these 'could' and 'would' phrases. Practise

00:06:10.992 --> 00:06:15.922
them after Lin and Victor.
Would you consider hedging against currency?

00:06:15.922 --> 00:06:25.501
Would you agree to hedging against currency?

00:06:25.501 --> 00:06:31.261
We could consider hedging against currency.

00:06:31.261 --> 00:06:40.951
We could agree to hedging against currency.
Sue comments: That would be acceptable. 'Acceptable'

00:06:40.951 --> 00:06:48.832
simply means able to be accepted. They can
agree to this solution. Practise with Sue

00:06:48.832 --> 00:06:54.781
some phrases you can use to agree, and disagree,
to conditions.

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:58.561
That would be acceptable.

00:06:58.561 --> 00:06:59.761
We can agree to that.

00:06:59.761 --> 00:07:03.651
We would be agreeable to that.

00:07:03.651 --> 00:07:10.622
That wouldn't be acceptable I'm afraid.

00:07:10.622 --> 00:07:15.061
I'm afraid we can't agree to that.

00:07:15.061 --> 00:07:22.041
We wouldn't be agreeable to that.
Notice that to 'agree with' someone, is to

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:28.981
think they are right. To 'agree to' something,
is to accept a proposal.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:34.992
What is Victor's second condition? Let's see.
If we do get large orders, we need to guarantee

00:07:34.992 --> 00:07:40.541
delivery - so we need to stockpile. The difficulty
there is the capital outlay. How would you

00:07:40.541 --> 00:07:47.522
feel about a partial offset against our sales?
You might use the phrase 'how would you feel

00:07:47.522 --> 00:07:51.622
about' something if you think it may be a
difficult condition for the other side to

00:07:51.622 --> 00:07:57.802
accept. Sue puts this proposal a different
way.

00:07:57.802 --> 00:08:02.811
Would you be agreeable to a deferred payment?
We can provide security of course.

00:08:02.811 --> 00:08:09.151
I think that would be acceptable. Unfortunately,
I would need to get Board approval for it.

00:08:09.151 --> 00:08:15.821
Sue uses the phrase would you be agreeable
: Would you be agreeable to a deferred payment?

00:08:15.821 --> 00:08:22.222
A deferred payment is when you pay later for
something you buy. Lin thinks this condition

00:08:22.222 --> 00:08:27.921
is acceptable, but she makes it conditional
on Board approval by saying, "I would need

00:08:27.921 --> 00:08:35.251
to get Board approval." Notice the stress
on would . Practise some phrases you can use

00:08:35.251 --> 00:08:43.580
for this type of condition.
That would have to have Board approval.

00:08:43.581 --> 00:08:48.711
The Board would need to approve that.

00:08:48.711 --> 00:08:58.182
That would be subject to Board approval.
Sometimes conditions depend on other conditions.

00:08:58.182 --> 00:09:03.081
One side might say, "We agree to your condition
if"

00:09:03.081 --> 00:09:08.761
So it's very important when negotiating to
listen for words that signal a condition:

00:09:08.761 --> 00:09:15.261
words like if, could, would,
provided or providing,

00:09:15.261 --> 00:09:18.922
as long as and subject to.

00:09:18.922 --> 00:09:22.162
And words that might signal a problem, such
as

00:09:22.162 --> 00:09:27.632
problem, unfortunately, and however .

00:09:27.632 --> 00:09:32.422
Notice that even at the end, they are being
careful about what they say.

00:09:32.422 --> 00:09:34.732
Then I think we might have a deal!

00:09:34.732 --> 00:09:40.232
In principle.
Lin agrees in principle. This means they have

00:09:40.232 --> 00:09:45.542
agreed on terms among themselves, but as she
needs Board approval, she can't authorise

00:09:45.542 --> 00:09:48.401
the agreement right now.

00:09:48.401 --> 00:09:53.372
And right now we've reached the end of today's
program. See you next time on the Business

00:09:53.372 --> 00:09:54.082
of English.